 going to shake you and you  can't take that personally that's what  they're supposed to do  they're supposed to test whether or not  it's a real  thing that's going on here so so that's  that's really important to know that  they're going to  test you and i guess the third  point to emphasize is you know sting  says if you love someone you gotta let  them go right  um as your kids get older you have to  give them more freedom  to be who they are they have to find  their friends and figure out what their  passions and interests are and it may  not be yours  and so i talk to teens all the time and  i say to them  you've got to help your parents let go  you have to let them know that you're  safe  by letting them know that you'll be a  home at a certain time and then come  home at a certain time  that's how you're preparing them for you  going off to college and becoming an  adult  because parenting is traumatizing  parenting is is absolutely traumatized i  mean in the middle of the night  we're up two in the morning holding our  child who who's  diarrheaed all over us and and and  thrown up on us and doing all this it's  traumatizing  so the by the time you have a teenager  we all have  parenting ptsd a little bit yeah and and  the way you deal with somebody with ptsd  is you give them assurances and not just  tell them but you show them  over and over it's going to be okay and  that's what i need teens to learn to do  to their parents  is to show us that it's going to be okay  so when it's time to let go  we as parents are able to do that it's a  two-way street  it's really great advice for parents  with teenagers because  you know it seems like the parents are  always setting the boundaries  but asking the teenagers to do that um  it's not a huge ask and uh yeah it  creates a great relationship with their  parents  that's great advice um my pithy answer  is this  we often don't discover our boundaries  until they've been crossed  so what i mean by that is there are