ith  that's it i'm taking away your phone for  the next three weeks well you know just  because they swore at me is that really  worth taking away their phone or through  maybe they swore at me because they're  really mad at me and maybe there's a  reason for them to be mad you know you  have to step back and see the big  picture  um but yeah they'll cross boundaries you  never even knew existed  yeah right now now is it true though  that we also we set up  boundaries that don't need to exist and  that can be  as big of a problem as not setting up  boundaries right  but by being too rigid in our thinking  and as you said  so eloquently i in fact sean another  pithy answer for you was about  when you're disappointed your kid you're  disappointed your own expectations right  and um that has to do with setting up  i'm not gonna say the wrong boundaries  but inappropriate boundaries  yeah don't chew with your mouth open  when you're two well don't don't get  don't get dirty when you're five right i  mean they're supposed to get dirty  um and so yeah we have to really think  about what's  what's the purpose of our boundary and  why are we setting limits here i  i tell when i talk to parent groups one  of the things i say is is there's  uh 5hs that kids have to do teens have  to do and if they're doing that  you got a pretty good kid and you're  going to be okay and that's take care of  your health  which is exercise sleep nutrition take  care of your hygiene  shower brush your teeth put on deodorant  take care of your homework  do your household chores and then hang  out  with your friends and if you're doing  those five things you're learning the  lessons  that you need in order to be successful  not you know why i've picked those is  because  none of those things really help kids in  the immediate uh  in the immediate doing your homework  that's not gonna help me now it's gonna  help me years down the road right  um wearing deodorant i can't smell my  own stink i don't